ing/data/ensemble/results.csv`). - **Nodes, not seconds**, for every hardness statement; seconds beside them.   A censored call (deadline) is a lower bound, never a missing value. - **State the size range every conclusion covers.** The corpus is 6,376   instances at 9–134 customers; the campaign is 37,800 at 10–40; item 02   extends it to 75 and, on the ridge, 100. Nothing below 100 is evidence about   125×125 except through a stated extrapolation. - Every number in a report is regenerated by a command named in that report. - Findings that rest on a handful of instances are re-certified for those   instances (`solve_mosp_exact`, never `solutions_dir=None`) before being written.  - **Compute**: check `pgrep -f benchmarks.recertify`; if it is running, its   cores are not yours. 32 cores total; use up to 16 here. An RTX 5090 with CUDA   is available. Each iteration has a hard 3-hour wall-clock cap enforced by the   driver; budget so the report section and PROGRESS.md are written well inside   it; the two long-running items (03, 04) price their runs with the ridge law   (§14) before starting them and sample if the full run will not fit, saying so.  ## Current Focus `reports/ml_nature_plan_2.md` §3: fourteen items in dependency order. The differential harness comes first because everything after it rests on the answers being sound.  ## Target Problems (in order) See `iterations.md`. Work on exactly ONE unchecked item per iteration.  ## Acceptance Criteria (per item) - [ ] Code lives under `learning/` (or `lean/` for item 12) with a module       docstring saying what question it answers and how to run it; runnable as       `python -m learning.<name>`. - [ ] A section appended to `reports/ml_nature.md` (continuing the numbering,       §15 onwards) with: the question, the method, the baseline, the table(s),       the finding in one paragraph, the size range covered, the kill criterion's       verdict where the plan states one, and the regenerate command. - [ ] `python -m py